<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<gpx version="1.1" creator="route-planner"
  xmlns="http://www.topografix.com/GPX/1/1"
  xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
  xsi:schemaLocation="http://www.topografix.com/GPX/1/1 http://www.topografix.com/GPX/1/1/gpx.xsd">
  <metadata>
    <name>Lakeland Way — Anticlockwise, over the top only</name>
    <desc>Base 208.3km plus 75.2km (+5615m ascent). Every summit and swim on this line is walked over, chained, or passed on the way — nothing doubles back.</desc>
  </metadata>
  <wpt lat="54.361363" lon="-3.297380">
    <ele>234.0</ele>
    <name>Devoke Water</name>
    <desc>The largest tarn in the district at 234m, alone on Birker Fell with no fells immediately around it. Big skies, no shelter, rarely anyone there. Detour: +3.3km, +0m ascent.</desc>
    <type>wild-swim-spots</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.390868" lon="-3.267857">
    <ele>55.0</ele>
    <name>Gill Force</name>
    <desc>Where the Esk narrows into a rock gorge below Doctor Bridge, with a deep green pool and slabs above it. Directly on the route in lower Eskdale. On the route.</desc>
    <type>wild-swim-spots</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.384022" lon="-3.272991">
    <ele>153.0</ele>
    <name>Stanley Force</name>
    <desc>A 20m fall in a wooded ravine off Eskdale, with a pool at its foot. The gorge path is slippery — care on the approach. On the route.</desc>
    <type>wild-swim-spots</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.376762" lon="-3.123790">
    <ele>796.0</ele>
    <name>Brim Fell 796m</name>
    <desc>A 796m grassy top on the ridge north of Coniston Old Man. Detour: +5.2km, +450m ascent.</desc>
    <type>peaks</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.374549" lon="-3.117781">
    <ele>546.0</ele>
    <name>Low Water</name>
    <desc>A shelf tarn at 546m directly below the summit of Coniston Old Man. Detour: +3.3km, +260m ascent.</desc>
    <type>wild-swim-spots</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.370486" lon="-3.122079">
    <ele>803.0</ele>
    <name>Coniston Old Man 803m</name>
    <desc>An 803m fell rising straight from the village, its flanks full of slate quarries and copper workings. Large summit cairn and a view to the sea. Detour: +5.0km, +507m ascent.</desc>
    <type>peaks</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.368980" lon="-3.131326">
    <ele>505.0</ele>
    <name>Goats Water</name>
    <desc>Squeezed between Dow Crag and Coniston Old Man at 505m, with a boulder shore beneath the crag. Sunless for much of the day. Detour: +3.8km, +199m ascent.</desc>
    <type>wild-swim-spots</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.360667" lon="-3.136620">
    <ele>565.0</ele>
    <name>Blind Tarn</name>
    <desc>A true corrie tarn at 565m on Dow Crag with no inlet or outlet stream — it is fed entirely by the fell around it. Detour: +0.9km, +54m ascent.</desc>
    <type>wild-swim-spots</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.370357" lon="-3.135929">
    <ele>778.0</ele>
    <name>Dow Crag 778m</name>
    <desc>A 778m summit perched above five great climbing buttresses dropping to Goats Water. The walk up is easy; the north face is not. Detour: +3.6km, +181m ascent.</desc>
    <type>peaks</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.386757" lon="-3.205679">
    <ele>653.0</ele>
    <name>Harter Fell (Eskdale) 653m</name>
    <desc>A 653m fell of rocky tors above Eskdale, and one of the best-shaped small fells in the district. The true summit needs a short easy scramble. Detour: +2.9km, +400m ascent.</desc>
    <type>peaks</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.427755" lon="-3.029350">
    <ele>56.0</ele>
    <name>Elter Water</name>
    <desc>Shallow and reed-fringed at 56m, with the Langdale Pikes filling the view. Warmest water on the route by some margin. Detour: +2.1km, +13m ascent.</desc>
    <type>wild-swim-spots</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.419412" lon="-3.037337">
    <ele>92.0</ele>
    <name>Colwith Force</name>
    <desc>A 15m fall in woodland on the River Brathay between Little and Great Langdale. Detour: +4.3km, +140m ascent.</desc>
    <type>wild-swim-spots</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.419693" lon="-3.066548">
    <ele>104.0</ele>
    <name>Little Langdale Tarn</name>
    <desc>A quiet valley tarn at 104m with a reedy shore; much less visited than Elter Water. Detour: +2.3km, +64m ascent.</desc>
    <type>wild-swim-spots</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.397342" lon="-3.079845">
    <ele>295.0</ele>
    <name>Tilberthwaite Gill Waterfall</name>
    <desc>Falls in a narrow mined ravine above Tilberthwaite, on the route to Wetherlam. On the route.</desc>
    <type>wild-swim-spots</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.372717" lon="-3.087524">
    <ele>149.0</ele>
    <name>Coniston Waterfall</name>
    <desc>Falls on Church Beck in the Coppermines valley above Coniston. On the route.</desc>
    <type>wild-swim-spots</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.384129" lon="-3.111418">
    <ele>413.0</ele>
    <name>Levers Water</name>
    <desc>A dammed tarn at 413m in the Coppermines valley above Coniston, surrounded by mine workings. Deep, clear and cold. Detour: +0.8km, +0m ascent.</desc>
    <type>wild-swim-spots</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.432689" lon="-2.951210">
    <ele>142.0</ele>
    <name>Stock Ghyll Force Waterfall</name>
    <desc>A 21m fall in woodland immediately above Ambleside. Detour: +1.7km, +61m ascent.</desc>
    <type>wild-swim-spots</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.452227" lon="-2.979696">
    <ele>146.0</ele>
    <name>High Fall</name>
    <desc>A fall in the Rydal Beck valley behind Rydal Hall. Detour: +0.7km, +35m ascent.</desc>
    <type>wild-swim-spots</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.449504" lon="-3.022421">
    <ele>64.0</ele>
    <name>Grasmere</name>
    <desc>Gentle shelving shore at 64m; busy in the day, calm early and late. Detour: +2.3km, +56m ascent.</desc>
    <type>wild-swim-spots</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.533258" lon="-3.009088">
    <ele>890.0</ele>
    <name>Catstye Cam 890m</name>
    <desc>A sharply conical 890m top just north of Helvellyn, often bypassed entirely despite being ten minutes off Swirral Edge. Detour: +7.2km, +643m ascent.</desc>
    <type>peaks</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.526890" lon="-3.018202">
    <ele>950.0</ele>
    <name>Helvellyn 950m</name>
    <desc>At 950m, the most-climbed high fell in the district, with a summit shelter and trig point. Approaching by Striding Edge and leaving by Swirral Edge is the classic round: both are Grade 1 scrambles with real exposure, and neither is sensible in high wind, ice or poor visibility. Detour: +9.2km, +706m ascent.</desc>
    <type>peaks</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.537865" lon="-2.953966">
    <ele>285.0</ele>
    <name>Lanty&apos;s Tarn</name>
    <desc>A small wooded tarn above Glenridding at 285m, dammed to supply ice to the estate. Sheltered and easy to reach — a good option in poor weather. On the route.</desc>
    <type>wild-swim-spots</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.507521" lon="-2.923946">
    <ele>161.0</ele>
    <name>Brothers Water</name>
    <desc>A small shallow water at 161m in Patterdale, with a reedy margin. Detour: +1.8km, +0m ascent.</desc>
    <type>wild-swim-spots</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.502068" lon="-2.879572">
    <ele>428.0</ele>
    <name>Hayeswater</name>
    <desc>A former reservoir at 428m under Gray Crag, now returned to a natural tarn. Detour: +0.3km, +0m ascent.</desc>
    <type>wild-swim-spots</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.506471" lon="-2.870977">
    <ele>739.0</ele>
    <name>The Knott 739m</name>
    <desc>A 739m rounded top at a junction of ridges above Hayeswater. On the route.</desc>
    <type>peaks</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.491239" lon="-2.864478">
    <ele>828.0</ele>
    <name>High Street 828m</name>
    <desc>An 828m whaleback carrying the line of a Roman road, with a wall along the top. Broad and easy going, and the route already crosses it. On the route.</desc>
    <type>peaks</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.488920" lon="-2.852351">
    <ele>486.0</ele>
    <name>Blea Water</name>
    <desc>The deepest tarn in England at around 63m, held in a textbook glacial corrie under High Street at 486m. Very cold, very clear, with a boulder shore and no easy shallows. Detour: +3.9km, +341m ascent.</desc>
    <type>wild-swim-spots</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.581691" lon="-2.929985">
    <ele>249.0</ele>
    <name>High Cascades</name>
    <desc>Upper falls in the Aira Beck glen. On the route.</desc>
    <type>wild-swim-spots</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.579303" lon="-2.929251">
    <ele>233.0</ele>
    <name>High Force</name>
    <desc>A fall in the Aira Beck glen above Ullswater, upstream of Aira Force. On the route.</desc>
    <type>wild-swim-spots</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.576634" lon="-2.930656">
    <ele>201.0</ele>
    <name>Aira Force</name>
    <desc>A 20m fall in a landscaped glen above Ullswater, crossed by two stone bridges. Popular and well made — not a swim, but a good stop. On the route.</desc>
    <type>wild-swim-spots</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.568648" lon="-3.170826">
    <ele>451.0</ele>
    <name>Cat Bells 451m</name>
    <desc>A 451m ridge above Derwent Water, out of all proportion in popularity to its size — a short, rocky, thoroughly enjoyable up-and-down with a superb view over the lake. Detour: +12.9km, +365m ascent.</desc>
    <type>peaks</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.504258" lon="-3.234004">
    <ele>487.0</ele>
    <name>Blackbeck Tarn</name>
    <desc>Just east of Innominate Tarn at 487m, a little larger and usually deserted. Detour: +2.9km, +208m ascent.</desc>
    <type>wild-swim-spots</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.505491" lon="-3.240770">
    <ele>528.0</ele>
    <name>Innominate Tarn</name>
    <desc>The tarn on Haystacks where Wainwright&apos;s ashes were scattered, at 528m. Small, peat-bedded and ringed by rock — the view down Ennerdale is the reason to stop. Detour: +3.4km, +261m ascent.</desc>
    <type>wild-swim-spots</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.506763" lon="-3.247822">
    <ele>597.0</ele>
    <name>Haystacks 597m</name>
    <desc>At 597m, Wainwright&apos;s favourite fell and where his ashes were scattered. A knot of small tops, tarns and rock — far more interesting than its modest height suggests. Detour: +6.3km, +504m ascent.</desc>
    <type>peaks</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.529919" lon="-3.265554">
    <ele>104.0</ele>
    <name>Buttermere</name>
    <desc>No motorised craft, ringed by Haystacks, Fleetwith Pike and High Stile. Easy grassy entry from the shore path near the Gatesgarth end. One of the finest lake swims on the route. On the route.</desc>
    <type>wild-swim-spots</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.527351" lon="-3.290136">
    <ele>493.0</ele>
    <name>Bleaberry Tarn</name>
    <desc>In the corrie between Red Pike and High Stile at 493m, above Buttermere. Detour: +3.5km, +392m ascent.</desc>
    <type>wild-swim-spots</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.541814" lon="-3.314638">
    <ele>243.0</ele>
    <name>Scale Force</name>
    <desc>The highest single drop in the Lake District at around 52m, in a mossy cleft above Crummock Water. The plunge pool is small and shaded. Detour: +2.1km, +109m ascent.</desc>
    <type>wild-swim-spots</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.459362" lon="-3.100687">
    <ele>475.0</ele>
    <name>Stickle Tarn</name>
    <desc>Dammed at 475m directly beneath the face of Pavey Ark, reached by a steep 40 minutes up Stickle Ghyll. The crag rising straight from the far shore is the draw. Detour: +6.6km, +491m ascent.</desc>
    <type>wild-swim-spots</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.480440" lon="-3.137482">
    <ele>209.0</ele>
    <name>Tray Dub</name>
    <desc>A quiet plunge pool on Langstrath Beck, a few metres off the path. On the route.</desc>
    <type>wild-swim-spots</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.481883" lon="-3.137750">
    <ele>203.0</ele>
    <name>Swan Dub</name>
    <desc>Small deep pool on Langstrath Beck, immediately beside the route. On the route.</desc>
    <type>wild-swim-spots</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.491915" lon="-3.132924">
    <ele>184.0</ele>
    <name>Black Moss Pot</name>
    <desc>A deep rock-walled pool in Langstrath, reached by a flat walk up the valley. Sheer sides give a natural jump of a few metres into water deep enough for it; a sloping slab at the downstream end is the easy way out. One of the best-known wild swims in the Lakes, and the route passes within 20m of it. On the route.</desc>
    <type>wild-swim-spots</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.508016" lon="-3.124697">
    <ele>124.0</ele>
    <name>Galleny Force</name>
    <desc>Known locally as the Fairy Glen — a chain of clear pools and small falls on Greenup Gill above Stonethwaite. Shallower and gentler than Black Moss Pot, with slabs to sit on. Directly on the route. On the route.</desc>
    <type>wild-swim-spots</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.519264" lon="-3.123420">
    <ele>411.0</ele>
    <name>Dock Tarn</name>
    <desc>A shallow tarn at 411m on the shelf above Stonethwaite, ringed by heather. Detour: +3.6km, +318m ascent.</desc>
    <type>wild-swim-spots</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.532783" lon="-3.162072">
    <ele>290.0</ele>
    <name>Castle Crag 290m</name>
    <desc>The only fell under 1000ft in the Wainwrights, at 290m. A quarried knoll in the Jaws of Borrowdale with a war memorial on top and a slate-spoil path. On the route.</desc>
    <type>peaks</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.522538" lon="-3.165788">
    <ele>167.0</ele>
    <name>Scaleclose Force</name>
    <desc>A fall on the beck below Honister, close to the route. Detour: +1.0km, +45m ascent.</desc>
    <type>wild-swim-spots</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.428301" lon="-3.259506">
    <ele>254.0</ele>
    <name>Burnmoor Tarn</name>
    <desc>A wide shallow tarn on the moor between Eskdale and Wasdale at 254m. Peaty and comparatively mild; the surrounding ground is boggy, so use the firmer northern shore. Detour: +0.0km, +0m ascent.</desc>
    <type>wild-swim-spots</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.472348" lon="-3.258305">
    <ele>116.0</ele>
    <name>Ritson Force</name>
    <desc>A short fall in woodland behind the Wasdale Head Inn, with a small pool beneath. Detour: +1.2km, +35m ascent.</desc>
    <type>wild-swim-spots</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.472261" lon="-3.219753">
    <ele>217.0</ele>
    <name>Emerald Pools</name>
    <desc>Green-tinted pools in the bed of Lingmell Beck below Wasdale Head, where the water has cut into clean rock. Cold and clear; easy entry from the shingle. Detour: +2.6km, +163m ascent.</desc>
    <type>wild-swim-spots</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.478084" lon="-3.202674">
    <ele>440.0</ele>
    <name>Styhead Tarn</name>
    <desc>Beneath Great Gable at 440m, right on the pass. Popular by day, quiet at either end of it. Mountain rescue stretcher box nearby. Detour: +1.4km, +49m ascent.</desc>
    <type>wild-swim-spots</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.488177" lon="-3.190660">
    <ele>302.0</ele>
    <name>Taylorgill Force</name>
    <desc>A 40m fall on Styhead Gill above Seathwaite, seen from the Styhead path. Detour: +4.3km, +204m ascent.</desc>
    <type>wild-swim-spots</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.471890" lon="-3.193005">
    <ele>602.0</ele>
    <name>Sprinkling Tarn</name>
    <desc>At 602m below Great End, one of the highest sizeable tarns in the district and a superb place to swim on a clear evening. Exposed — there is no shelter if the weather turns. On the route.</desc>
    <type>wild-swim-spots</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.454098" lon="-3.212285">
    <ele>978.0</ele>
    <name>Scafell Pike 978m</name>
    <desc>England&apos;s highest ground at 978m. A boulder-strewn summit plateau with a huge cairn; cairned throughout but genuinely serious in mist, where the plateau defeats casual navigation. Usual approach from the route is via Lingmell Col. Detour: +5.9km, +474m ascent.</desc>
    <type>peaks</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.458658" lon="-3.167308">
    <ele>557.0</ele>
    <name>Angle Tarn</name>
    <desc>Sitting at 557m under Bowfell and Rossett Pike, dark and deep with a boulder shore. Reached directly by the route over Rossett Gill. Detour: +0.0km, +0m ascent.</desc>
    <type>wild-swim-spots</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.444250" lon="-3.161208">
    <ele>724.0</ele>
    <name>Three Tarns</name>
    <desc>A cluster of small pools on the col at 724m between Bowfell and Crinkle Crags — more a place to dip than to swim, and utterly exposed. Detour: +4.4km, +470m ascent.</desc>
    <type>wild-swim-spots</type>
  </wpt>
  <trk>
    <name>Lakeland Way (over the top)</name>
    <trkseg>
      <trkpt lat="54.355452" lon="-3.408099"><ele>12.0</ele></trkpt>
